        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 16,
        "titulo": "What you can do at A1: the map",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: A1 is the first level of the European framework. It does not mean «knowing a lot of grammar»: it means doing a short list of real things. STEP 2: greet and be polite: «Buongiorno, signora!» (formal) and «ciao» (informal). STEP 3: say who you are and how old you are: «Mi chiamo Ana», «Ho venti anni». Remember that Italian says «I have twenty years», not «I am twenty». STEP 4: ask where something is and understand directions: «Dov'è la stazione», «Giri a destra», «Vada dritto fino al semaforo». STEP 5: order and ask for the bill: «Vorrei un cappuccino», «Il conto, per favore». STEP 6: say the time and the date: «Sono le tre», «Il compleanno è il 15 maggio». STEP 7: say what you want, can and must do: «Non posso venire stasera», «Devo studiare domani». STEP 8: say what you like and tell a simple past event: «Mi piace leggere», «Ho letto un libro». STEP 9: this is the whole map. In the next three sessions you read, listen, avoid the classic mistakes and take a mock test in the A1 format.",
        "comparacao": "English speakers usually feel strongest on vocabulary, because many Italian words look like English ones. The map shows where the real work is: small structure words and verbs, not nouns. Age, hunger and cold use «avere», not «essere». Negation puts «non» before the verb. Possessives usually take an article (except with a singular family member: «mia sorella»). Prepositions merge with the article («alla stazione»). If you check these four things, most A1 sentences are right.",
